my power for the computer at home. So hopefully it will last the entire semester. If not, we'll just have to deal with an imperfect scenario, but that's okay. I think it must be something subconscious because it's too much of a coincidence

17
00:03:05,010 --> 00:03:09,730
that every time something happens, walk the talk.

18
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:12,630
So where were we last time?

19
00:03:13,110 --> 00:03:17,890
Last time we talked about how you learned how to walk.

20
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:24,690
We learned about how you learned how to draw circles, and all through failure.

21
00:03:25,430 --> 00:03:35,450
As Thomas Edison said, I learned my way, I failed my way to success. And we saw it with top athletes, whether it was Babe Ruth or Michael Jordan.

22
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:41,290
We saw it with top business people, whether it was Thomas Watson, IBM,

23
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:46,490
or Anita Roddick, founder of the body shop.

24
00:03:48,130 --> 00:03:56,970
We see it with artists and scientists. The most successful ones are also the ones who failed the most. and these are the ones who understand the value and the importance of failure.

25
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:00,270
I guarantee you that none of them enjoy failing.

26
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:02,110
Failing hurts.

27
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:04,930
It's disappointing at best.

28
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:07,630
It's frustrating.

29
00:04:07,990 --> 00:04:10,090
It's embarrassing often.

30
00:04:10,510 --> 00:04:16,670
However, people who ultimately succeed recognize the value of failure and learning from it.

31
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:30,200
There's a wonderful book that came out recently by one of my mentors. He taught at the business school for a few years. He's a professor at the University of Southern California, Robert Warren Bennis.

32
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:35,740
And Warren Bennis wrote a book which he called Geeks and Geezers.

33
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:43,820
And in the book, he compared very successful leaders who were young, early 30s,

34
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:53,460
and older generation leaders, people who have really made it, big-time leaders who are now in their 70s, 80s, sometimes 90s.

35
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:58,580
And he compared these two groups, the geeks, the young ones, the geezers, the older ones.

36
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:07,620
And what he found was that there were a number of very interesting and meaningful differences

37
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:17,640
between the generations of, again, highly successful late 20s or early 30-year-olds and highly successful older individuals.

38
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:23,420
One of the most notable differences was that work-life balance,

39
00:05:24,780 --> 00:05:35,020
which was very important for the late 20s, early 30s, very important. They talked about it constantly, whereas for the 70, 80, 90-year-olds,

40
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:38,400
it was almost a foreign concept. What does that even mean?

41
00:05:38,980 --> 00:05:42,900
Why? Because they were at work usually 24-7.

42
00:05:43,960 --> 00:05:49,580
All of them in that sample were men, and the women were at home taking care of the life part.

43
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:53,180
So there was a work-life balance where they were doing the work,

44
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:56,080
the wife was doing the life.

45
00:05:57,280 --> 00:06:05,200
Whereas the younger generation, men and women, in that sample, and talking about the work-life balance and a few other differences as well.

46
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:09,700
But there was one similarity, one thing that was common to both groups,

47
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:17,320
and that was at least one, usually more than one, significant failure.

48
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:22,640
What Warren Bennis came to call a crucible, a real crisis,

49
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:28,560
whether it was a meaningful loss or losing a job, being fired,

50
00:06:31,010 --> 00:06:36,820
one's identity being assaulted, loss of a person,

51
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:40,160
some real serious crucible, a failure.

52
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:53,220
That was common to both groups, and both groups saw that failure as a pivotal point, as a turning point in their lives, something that helped them become who they are today.

53
00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:59,200
Again, not saying it happened for the best, but they were able to make the best of that which happened.

54
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:11,720
Now, most people experience crucibles throughout their lives, hardships, difficulties. The difference between the highly successful people, for example, the sample in Warren Bennis' book,

55
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:21,740
and many other people is that they're able to make the best of what happens. They see it as an opportunity, a learning experience, a stepping stone,

56
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:30,960
the importance of failure for resilience, for well-being as well.

57
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:42,240
So let me move on and define what we mean by perfectionism because there are many definitions and we throw that word around constantly. But what do I actually mean by perfectionism?

58
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:52,420
I define it as an incapacitating fear of failure that permeates our lives, especially those areas that we care about most.

59
00:07:53,100 --> 00:08:03,380
Note the word incapacitating. It is not just a fear of failure. I don't know a person who is not afraid of failure, who is not embarrassed by it or feels frustrated by it.

60
00:08:04,540 --> 00:08:08,180
So that's natural, that's human, whether we like it or not.

61
00:08:08,630 --> 00:08:19,260
But an incapacitating fear of failure, a fear that is debilitating, that stops us, that prevents us from coping, from trying, from putting ourselves on the line.

62
00:08:22,430 --> 00:08:28,030
And the other part that I want to highlight in this definition is that especially those areas that we care about most.

63
00:08:28,730 --> 00:08:34,450
Now, I'm not a perfectionist when it comes to playing Monopoly.

64
00:08:35,390 --> 00:08:39,950
No, it's not that I'm competitive, but it's not that important for me if I win or lose.

65
00:08:41,110 --> 00:08:52,670
But perfectionism was a central part of my life when it came to, as I mentioned earlier, squash, which was very meaningful to me, academics, which is very meaningful to me,

66
00:08:53,550 --> 00:08:58,430
and relationships, which was and is a very important part of my life.

67
00:08:59,830 --> 00:09:05,850
So in these areas, that's where I experienced perfectionism, and that's where I needed to do the work, and I'm still doing the work.

68
00:09:06,370 --> 00:09:09,970
It's in those areas. Let me elaborate on this definition.

69
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:20,890
It is an approach, a cognitive and emotional schema that we hold towards the journey of our lives, towards the process of getting from point A to point B.

70
00:09:21,170 --> 00:09:24,110
The emphasis here is on journey.

71
00:09:24,610 --> 00:09:35,950
A person committed to excellence can still be as ambitious or more ambitious than a perfectionist. The difference between the two is just their approach toward the journey. Let me illustrate.

72
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:43,090
So let's first take the perfectionist.

73
00:09:43,950 --> 00:09:50,570
The perfectionist is currently at point A, wants to get to point B.

74
00:09:51,370 --> 00:09:55,750
What's the perfect, most efficient path between two points?

75
00:09:56,330 --> 00:09:57,270
Straight line.

76
00:09:57,890 --> 00:10:08,750
This is the path, this is the cognitive and emotional schema that the perfectionist has when he or she looks at the journey,

77
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:13,570
how they want to get from where they are now to where they want to get to.

78
00:10:14,330 --> 00:10:24,930
A person committed to excellence, as ambitious as the person, the perfectionist,

79
00:10:24,930 --> 00:10:30,130
wants to get from point A to point B. No different here. What is different?

80
00:10:30,990 --> 00:10:42,570
The cognitive schema that relates to the journey. The person committed to excellence understands that while he improves, inevitably at some point he will fail.

81
00:10:43,410 --> 00:10:56,460
A perfectionist understands that sometimes she may not get the grade that she expected, but she will learn from it.

82
00:10:56,760 --> 00:11:08,520
A perfectionist understands that in relationships there is no such thing as a perfect relationship and therefore he sometimes makes a mistake

83
00:11:08,520 --> 00:11:10,520
or she sometimes makes a mistake.

84
00:11:11,720 --> 00:11:20,420
But they continue and they learn from it and the relationship gets stronger. And a perfectionist understands that you need to fail time

85
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:27,660
and again, five times, ten times, sometimes ten thousand times as we saw with Edison

86
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:32,960
until they get to point B.

87
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:43,100
as ambitious but the schema is very different you have to draw many failed circles before you get

88
00:11:44,280 --> 00:11:55,260
to that ideal circle that vincent drew last time and the question now becomes

89
00:11:56,580 --> 00:12:04,900
so which is realistic is it realistic to have that schema or is this one more realistic well

90
00:12:04,900 --> 00:12:11,080
I think it's pretty obvious. There is no straight line towards success. There is no straight line

91
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:18,100
towards a happy relationship. There is no straight line towards inventing the light bulb or being

92
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:27,600
good at psychology or being a good parent or a good friend. We make mistakes and nature to be

93
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:34,760
commanded must be obeyed. Pursuing excellence is about the constrained view of nature.

94
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:41,120
Yes, I wish that I could get from point A to point B in this way. I wish it were so.

95
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:47,960
I didn't enjoy failing my generals. I didn't enjoy falling down many times and losing in squash.

96
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:52,020
I certainly don't enjoy having disagreements with my wife.

97
00:12:56,020 --> 00:13:01,100
However, I also would rather not have the law of gravity and be able to fly everywhere or float everywhere.

98
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:07,140
This is. This is a fantasy.

99
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:14,380
And when we have this schema in our mind, we're putting ourselves, fighting nature.

100
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:22,780
And just like we would lead an unhappy and frustrating life if we did not accept the law of gravity, we live.

101
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:33,620
And so many people, if so many people at Harvard, not just at Harvard, everywhere, are frustrated constantly because in their minds they have this schema.

102
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:36,920
And this does not mean that we enjoy failure.

103
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:47,860
But when we have this schema, in addition to not enjoying failure, we have the frustration of not accepting reality.

104
00:13:51,390 --> 00:13:53,670
Now, there are consequences to having this schema.

105
00:13:54,770 --> 00:14:02,210
I want to discuss some of these consequences and some of the characteristics of a person who holds this schema.

106
00:14:02,570 --> 00:14:07,250
Now, there is no perfect perfectionist, as I said, and we all exist somewhere along the continuum.

107
00:14:07,930 --> 00:14:15,170
There are many people, again, many people here, who are closer to this end of the continuum.

108
00:14:19,030 --> 00:14:25,270
The characteristics that I'd like to share with you are all ones that are documented in literature.

109
00:14:25,690 --> 00:14:33,550
There are many people who have them. You will identify with some of the characteristics, not with others, and that's fine.

110
00:14:34,250 --> 00:14:41,610
All the characteristics, in addition to being well documented in the psychological literature, are also ones that I've experienced or still experience.

111
00:14:42,690 --> 00:14:52,550
So these ones are personally meaningful to me, in addition to being also quite common. The first one, defensiveness.

112
00:14:55,720 --> 00:15:07,000
In arguments, in discussions, perfectionists are defensive. Why? Because the criticism is by definition a deviation from the straight line.

113
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:10,600
It's an imperfection. It's a dent.

114
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:13,900
It's something that maybe I'm not doing perfectly well.

115
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:24,240
And we don't like deviations from a schema that we have. Remember, the mind doesn't like when there is an inconsistency between what is inside and what is outside.

116
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:28,700
We want there to be a balance, congruence. We don't like dissonance.

117
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:35,440
And if what we have in mind is the perfectionist schema, then a criticism is a deviation from it.

118
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:37,420
And then we become defensive.

119
00:15:37,800 --> 00:15:42,580
Again, something that I've experienced numerous times and something that I'm working on constantly.

120
00:15:43,820 --> 00:15:46,260
In addition, or in contrast,

121
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:57,660
a person committed to excellence is open, is welcoming of suggestions and of criticism at times. It doesn't mean that he or she enjoys it, but he or she understands that it's necessary,

122
00:15:57,880 --> 00:16:01,140
that it's an important part of growth, of development.

123
00:16:03,690 --> 00:16:10,090
A perfectionist focuses on the half-empty part of the glass. Why? Because a perfectionist is obsessed by failure,

124
00:16:10,970 --> 00:16:18,750
Obsessed by deviations from that straight and narrow. And whatever it is that we're obsessed with by definition is what we constantly focus on.

125
00:16:19,250 --> 00:16:27,110
So he or she is constantly focused on what is not working, on potential failures or actual failures.

126
00:16:29,380 --> 00:16:41,360
Person committed to excellence focuses on the half-full part of the glass. How? Because he or she enjoys getting to wherever he or she is going, but also learns to enjoy every step of the way.

127
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:51,700
the journey. Even failure is an opportunity. A perfectionist overgeneralizes, catastrophizes.

128
00:16:51,960 --> 00:17:01,160
It's all or nothing. Either I'm perfect or I'm a failure. And we'll see the consequences in a minute of this all or nothing approach.

129
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:12,960
versus realistic again realizing what reality looks like what progress in

130
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:21,140
essence looks like for a perfectionist there is no self-acceptance a person

131
00:17:21,140 --> 00:17:30,500
committed to excellence there is acceptance first of all of reality as comprising spirals rather than a straight line acceptance of personal

132
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:40,020
failure and acceptance of the self as a whole. Perfectionist, there is one way and that's

133
00:17:40,020 --> 00:17:46,560
the highway. It is static. There is no improvisation. There is no deviation. There is no trial and

134
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:54,200
error. There is just one way in contrast to having a much more dynamic, flexible process

135
00:17:54,200 --> 00:18:01,500
and journey with much more spontaneity, which manifests itself, of course, in behaviors.

136
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:27,280
A perfectionist is terrified of failure, terrified that failure from within, that he or she perceives themselves as failures, but also that others see him or her as a failure, keeping that facade, that image of impregnability, of perfection versus failure as feedback.

137
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:34,860
Again, not enjoying failure. I haven't met a person who does enjoy failing, but seeing it more as feedback as a growth opportunity.

138
00:18:35,960 --> 00:18:41,960
A perfectionist, it's all about getting here. That's what it's about.

139
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:50,820
This is a means toward an end, tangential, something that is not important other than taking me from here to here.

140
00:18:50,820 --> 00:18:57,940
Whereas for a person committed to excellence, the journey is as much part of success as the destination.

141
00:19:01,010 --> 00:19:10,250
Now, there are numerous, numerous consequences to this distinction, to holding one schema versus the other.

142
00:19:10,630 --> 00:19:11,810
Here are some of them.

143
00:19:12,450 --> 00:19:21,450
The first one is that a perfectionist experiences only, at best, temporary relief.

144
00:19:21,450 --> 00:19:27,470
So yes, there is constant pressure, but when he or she gets to point B, then they feel relieved.

145
00:19:28,730 --> 00:19:33,150
This is essentially the consummate rat racer.

146
00:19:35,730 --> 00:19:39,930
Let me give you an example of such a rat racer.

147
00:19:44,980 --> 00:19:57,460
The person, let's call him X, goes to school, goes to elementary school.

148
00:19:58,300 --> 00:20:05,880
Until that point, he really enjoyed his daycare, pre-K, kindergarten.

149
00:20:06,720 --> 00:20:10,500
Then he goes to school and the pressure begins.

150
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:23,260
Because he begins to understand that he really needs to work very hard and do extremely well if he is to get into a good middle school.

151
00:20:24,080 --> 00:20:27,480
because the middle school that his parents want him to go to

152
00:20:27,980 --> 00:20:32,700
is very competitive, very tough to get into. And he did get into a very good elementary school,

153
00:20:33,180 --> 00:20:37,640
but now he has to do the same for middle school. So the pressure mounts.

154
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:41,480
And he doesn't really enjoy school,

155
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:47,200
but then he experiences relief when the holidays come

156
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:49,680
and when they go on a family vacation

157
00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:55,820
or when the exams end and he can play with his friends without thinking of the next test.

158
00:20:57,360 --> 00:21:01,460
He goes through school not really liking it, experiencing more a series of relief

159
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:06,280
rather than passion and love of learning, which he did experience when he was in kindergarten,

160
00:21:07,040 --> 00:21:16,100
but no more. And then he gets into his dream middle school, the middle school that he was meant to go to all along. He makes it.

161
00:21:16,500 --> 00:21:19,240
And he feels wonderful and great and terrific

162
00:21:19,960 --> 00:21:25,820
for two weeks because he now needs to start thinking about the high school of his dreams.

163
00:21:26,620 --> 00:21:33,400
Why? Because that high school is a feeder school for the top colleges in the country,

164
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:36,080
and he really wants to get into one of those.

165
00:21:37,700 --> 00:21:43,580
So he really struggles and works hard in middle school, doesn't really enjoy his work,

166
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:49,520
but he makes it and he gets into his dream high school, and he is ecstatic.

167
00:21:50,280 --> 00:21:58,820
happier than he'd ever been before because here he is among the smartest kids in the country very

168
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:08,820
difficult to get into the school but he makes it and there he starts to learn he's happy for about a week and then the pressure mounts because it is a very competitive school and he has to join

169
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:16,860
two varsity sports and three student organizations because he has to pad to build his resume so that

170
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:25,520
he gets in to his dream college he struggles and it's not fun but he says to himself this is just

171
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:31,780
temporary it's only pain now because later there will be gain once i get into my top college

172
00:22:31,780 --> 00:22:45,320
and he applies and on april 2nd there is an envelope that arrived and the envelope is large

173
00:22:45,320 --> 00:22:52,060
and he opens it up and he gets in and he's ecstatic happier even than he was when he got

174
00:22:52,060 --> 00:23:03,340
to his dream high school and he says now finally I can let go now finally I can relax because I'm already made I mean this top college and I'm set for life

175
00:23:04,820 --> 00:23:10,960
and he really enjoys the rest of senior year and the holiday and then

176
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:22,960
freshman week comes and he's ecstatic Mr. X but then after that week the pressure mounts again because very soon there are midterms and very soon there

177
00:23:22,960 --> 00:23:31,550
is competition and everyone is working as hard as he did and he has to work

178
00:23:31,550 --> 00:23:39,340
really hard because he wants to get his dream job and then he gets his ideal

179
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:50,840
internship during freshman summer and the pressure continues there are relief there are fun periods and these are periods when the exam is just over or

180
00:23:50,840 --> 00:24:01,240
when break is coming up or during break, but then again, the pressure mounts. He doesn't

181
00:24:01,240 --> 00:24:12,140
really understand why he's not happy because this was where he really wanted to be, really, really wanted to be more than any other place. But he says, okay, once I get that job, then

182
00:24:12,140 --> 00:24:18,820
I'll be set. Why? Because that job will really get me into the top business school, will

183
00:24:18,820 --> 00:24:24,860
really differentiate me from all the other 1,600 students who will graduate with me.

184
00:24:25,900 --> 00:24:36,530
So he works hard, still builds his resume. He's a member of three student organizations, president of two, JV in two sports and varsity

185
00:24:36,530 --> 00:24:47,910
in one, plus taking five and sometimes six courses a semester. Why? Because it's tough, it's competitive, and it's a survival of the fittest, and no pain,

186
00:24:48,050 --> 00:24:48,730
no gain.

187
00:24:49,630 --> 00:24:54,430
Senior year finally comes, and there is pressure, but then he gets his job.

188
00:24:55,650 --> 00:25:00,250
He's happy. This is exactly what he wanted. This is exactly what he envisioned.

189
00:25:01,770 --> 00:25:03,090
And now he's happy.

190
00:25:04,330 --> 00:25:14,630
And then comes August 3rd, which is the date when he starts his actual work. And he goes to work, and everyone around him, he's a graduate of top universities, top places,

191
00:25:14,630 --> 00:25:24,350
he feels on top of the world and he's happy for two weeks because after two

192
00:25:24,350 --> 00:25:31,790
weeks he realized that there is a lot of pressure here actually much more than there was in college and he can't have time for those long leisurely dinners

193
00:25:32,330 --> 00:25:42,070
